["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"ADHD Medication Changes Behavior, Not Learning Barkley likens ADHD to diabetes, yet 64% of children move in and out of the diagnostic criteria repeatedly; medication buys compliance without improving learning, the long-term MTA follow-up confirms no academic advantage, and what actually works is small-group differentiated teaching. Your Parenting Mojo Your Parenting Mojo 266: If ADHD Medication Doesn’t Help Kids Learn, What Does? ADHD Child education Drug efficacy Evidence-based teaching Behavioral intervention MTA MTA study crossover design DSM misrecognition\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-06-01-parentmojo-adhd-药物改得了行为-改不了学习/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/70c340acb32275d2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">30:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Your Parenting Mojo</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0601</span></div>\n<h2>ADHD Medication Changes Behavior, Not Learning</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Barkley likens ADHD to diabetes, yet 64% of children move in and out of the diagnostic criteria repeatedly; medication buys compliance without improving learning, the long-term MTA follow-up confirms no academic advantage, and what actually works is small-group differentiated teaching.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">ADHD</span><span class=\"tag\">Child education</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"14,000 cars a year, worth more than Ford: Ferrari sells exclusivity and inclusion at once Ferrari builds only about 14,000 cars a year, yet its market value exceeds Ford&#x27;s. The trick is holding a luxury brand&#x27;s exclusivity and a sports team&#x27;s inclusiveness at the same time: scarcity manufactures the myth, community sustains the loyalty. Acquired Acquired Ferrari Ferrari Luxury goods Scarcity Brand Business model Motor racing Tifosi Classiche Purosangue\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-04-13-acquired-法拉利-卖的不是车-是排他与包容的悖论/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b28a1307f172ac6a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">3:59:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Acquired</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0413</span></div>\n<h2>14,000 cars a year, worth more than Ford: Ferrari sells exclusivity and inclusion at once</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ferrari builds only about 14,000 cars a year, yet its market value exceeds Ford&#x27;s. The trick is holding a luxury brand&#x27;s exclusivity and a sports team&#x27;s inclusiveness at the same time: scarcity manufactures the myth, community sustains the loyalty.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ferrari</span><span class=\"tag\">Luxury goods</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Program Equilibrium: Trading Code Cooperates More Than Trading People, but Coordination Is Extremely Hard Program equilibrium lets two parties read each other&#x27;s source code and achieve cooperation via Löb&#x27;s theorem; but cooperation depends on nearly identical code or shared randomness, and in reality coordination costs are extremely high. AXRP AXRP 49 - Caspar Oesterheld on Program Equilibrium Game Theory AI Alignment Program Equilibrium Multi-Agent Mechanism Design program equilibrium Löb&#x27;s theorem folk theorem epsilon grounded fairbot simulationist programs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-02-18-axrp-程序均衡-交代码比交人更可能合作-但协调极难/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/cd45e667daa75191-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">2:32:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AXRP</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0218</span></div>\n<h2>Program Equilibrium: Trading Code Cooperates More Than Trading People, but Coordination Is Extremely Hard</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Program equilibrium lets two parties read each other&#x27;s source code and achieve cooperation via Löb&#x27;s theorem; but cooperation depends on nearly identical code or shared randomness, and in reality coordination costs are extremely high.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Game Theory</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Alignment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Giving AI Property Rights Is How You Stop It From Flipping the Table If AI owns property, it has less incentive to destroy the property system itself — including taking everything humans own. This isn&#x27;t moral concern; it&#x27;s risk hedging. AXRP AXRP 48 - Guive Assadi on AI Property Rights AI governance property rights long-term risk alignment historical analogy rentier racing to the precipice Roko&#x27;s basilisk Laplace&#x27;s rule of succession\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-02-15-axrp-给-ai-产权-是为了防止它掀桌/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8a8ac42151ce3e12-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">2:05:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AXRP</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0215</span></div>\n<h2>Giving AI Property Rights Is How You Stop It From Flipping the Table</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">If AI owns property, it has less incentive to destroy the property system itself — including taking everything humans own. This isn&#x27;t moral concern; it&#x27;s risk hedging.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span><span class=\"tag\">property rights</span>\n</div></div></a>"]